Seed the table from a snapshot when the source is replaced wholesale

TRUNCATE, RESTORE and BENCH replace the whole table, and the engine
reports each as one RESET frame carrying no operations - a deliberate
design, stated in cdc.inc and parse.inc. A consumer that met one was
stuck for ever: the change log holds no rows for the new contents, so
replaying it clears the image and leaves nothing. Refusing was correct
but permanent.

The engine already carried what a seed needs. HDR_SEQ at header offset
88 is the last commit sequence published to the change log, so a
snapshot can be pinned to an exact point in the log; that is what makes
"seed, then resume incrementally" correct rather than approximate.

The gateway now serves GET /snapshot/{instanceId}. It reads the header
before and after the scan and refuses rather than serve a torn image:
503 while a bulk operation is in flight, 409 if the sequence moved. The
notebook seeds from it on RESET, writes the snapshot sequence as its
watermark, and resumes at that sequence in the same run.

Two faults found in review rather than in testing, because both only
appear at scale. The seed accumulated every row in driver memory before
writing anything, against a table whose capacity is over three million
rows; it now stages page by page and swaps once, still replacing data
before the watermark. And the scan was bounded by rows found rather than
slots examined, which matters because the engine reserves the file up
front and writes sparsely - the live instance holds 2.44 MiB of data in
a 1 GiB reservation, so a snapshot of an empty table would have read a
gigabyte looking for rows that were not there. It is now bounded per
page and ends early when the header's live count says the table is
empty: measured at 1.1 seconds against the real file.

Reseeding is capped per run, so a source being replaced faster than it
can be followed fails with that explanation instead of rewriting the
lakehouse table in a loop.

The preview no longer presents dead rows as current. A change log keeps
its history, so after a TRUNCATE it happily returned six frames of rows
that no longer exist, with no hint that two RESETs had invalidated them,
and drew the decoder sample from the oldest of them. It now reports the
log's range, names the newest RESET, strikes through superseded entries
and leaves the sample empty rather than offer a ghost.

